#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""fm-memory-pointers - index firstmate's canonical files as pointer memories.

`AGENTS.md` section 6 makes data/captain.md, data/learnings.md and
data/projects.md canonical for their domains, so the memory bridge indexes them
by POINTER and never by copy: each memory is short enough to act on and names
the file and section that own the fact. The canonical file always wins.

Written in Python for the same reason bin/fm-memory-mcp is: it speaks that
server's stdio JSON-RPC. Every write goes through the bridge, so pinning, the
global scope, the lane routing, and every typed refusal are the bridge's, not a
second implementation of them. Nothing here opens a store.

Usage:
  fm-memory-pointers plan  [--home H] [--data-dir D]
      Derive the pointer set and print it. Opens no store and writes nothing.
  fm-memory-pointers write [--home H] [--data-dir D]
      Write every pointer through fm-memory-mcp and report the counts.
  fm-memory-pointers --help

SOURCES AND ROUTING
  data/captain.md   one pointer per `## ` section  -> lane 'shared', '_lane'
  data/learnings.md one pointer per `## ` entry    -> lane 'fleet-infra', '_lane'
  data/projects.md  one pointer per project        -> that project's own lane,
                    channel_id = the registered project name
  A project's lane comes from bin/fm-project-mode.sh --lane, which owns the
  registry line format. A project with no lane token is reported unrouted and
  skipped: this never guesses a lane, because a wrong lane is the cross-lane
  leak the whole model exists to prevent.

CLASSIFICATION (report section 3.5)
  captain decision   importance 0.9   veracity stated  type decision
  captain preference importance 0.85  veracity stated  type preference
  fleet learning     importance 0.6   veracity tool    type learning
  project pointer    importance 0.7   veracity stated  type context
  A captain section is a decision when its heading says so ('captain-decided',
  'captain-validated', 'restated', 'conditional'), and a preference otherwise.
  0.7 is deliberate for projects: it is the cross-lane awareness threshold, so
  every lane can see which projects exist elsewhere without reading a body.

IDEMPOTENCE
  Re-running writes no duplicates. Content is derived deterministically, so an
  unchanged section produces the exact text already stored and the store's own
  dedupe returns the same row: reported `created: false` and counted skipped.
  A section that MOVED says the same thing, so the line number is deliberately
  outside both the stored text and the digest: it lives in
  `metadata.canonical_line` alone, and a pure line shift is absorbed by the
  same dedupe as an untouched section. Rewriting a moved section would retire
  its row, and the store refuses to write text a retired row already holds -
  so reverting the edit that moved it could never be indexed again.
  A section that CHANGED produces different text, which would otherwise leave
  two live pointers for one fact. So a ledger beside each bank records the
  memory id, canonical digest, and canonical line per pointer, and a changed
  section is written as a supersession of the recorded row instead of a second
  memory.
  The ledger is derived, never authoritative: if the recorded row is gone or
  already retired the bridge refuses the supersession, and the pointer is
  written fresh. Deleting the ledger costs one extra pointer per changed
  section, never a wrong answer - except that reconciliation below has nothing
  left to work from, so pointers to sections deleted while the ledger was gone
  stay live until their key comes back.
  A pointer whose canonical section is EDITED is superseded on the next run,
  not on the edit. Between the two the store's text is stale while the digest
  in its metadata still names what it was derived from, which is what makes the
  drift detectable rather than silent.
  `metadata.canonical_line` is accurate as of the run that CREATED the row.
  The store's dedupe updates neither content nor metadata_json when it matches,
  so a pointer whose section only moved keeps the line it was written with; the
  ledger beside the bank carries the current line, and the anchor in the
  content is what finds the section either way.

RECONCILIATION
  Every run also retires what the canonical files no longer say. A ledger key
  the current derivation does not produce - a renamed heading, a deleted
  section, or a repeat renumbered when an earlier same-named section went away
  - is expired through the bridge's memory_expire, never by a direct write and
  never by deleting a row. Otherwise a withdrawn captain decision stays live
  and recall keeps returning it.
  Reconciliation visits every lane whose bank holds a ledger, not only the
  lanes that still derive pointers: a lane emptied to its last project - by a
  re-lane or by dropping the line - derives nothing, and walking only the
  derived set would leave its rows claiming a project that lives elsewhere now.
  A key is only retired when the source that owns it derived normally. A
  canonical file that is absent, unreadable, or READABLE BUT EMPTY yields no
  sections, which is indistinguishable here from every one of its sections
  being deleted, so ITS keys are left exactly as they are while every other
  source's keys reconcile as usual. A refusal costs one re-run; a wrongly
  retired pointer can never be written again, so the two are not weighed the
  same.
  A source that derived nothing is only a problem when a ledger still holds its
  keys, because that is the only case with anything to lose. data/learnings.md
  is created lazily, so a home that has recorded no learning yet derives none,
  reports none, and runs normally.
  Only a WHOLLY empty source is caught this way. A canonical file truncated
  part of the way through still derives the sections that survived, and those
  read exactly like deletions of the rest, so a partial loss is still
  reconciled and its missing pointers are still retired. Telling that apart
  from real deletions is not possible from here, and it is an accepted bound.
  Each ledger records the home its pointers were derived from, and a lane whose
  ledger names another home neither retires from nor supersedes through it: a
  derivation from another tree does not produce this one's pointers either, and
  chaining onto a row this run did not create retires it exactly as
  irreversibly as expiring it would. Such a run writes plainly, and the ledger
  keeps the home it was written from rather than being rebound. A ledger
  written before homes were recorded is adopted, retiring nothing on the run
  that adopts it.
  Anything left alone is reported under `skipped_reconciliation`, and the run
  exits nonzero: a run that deliberately did less than it was asked must never
  look like one that did everything.
  A key whose row this run rebound to a surviving pointer is dropped from the
  ledger rather than expired, because that row is the survivor.
  Expiry respects the same isolation as writing: a lane whose bridge will not
  start reconciles nothing, and a key it could not reconcile STAYS in the
  ledger, because that record holds the only id that can retire it later.

  Emptying a canonical file is therefore NOT a way to retire its pointers: it
  is refused, not obeyed. A wholesale retirement needs its own deliberate act.

KNOWN BOUND
  An edit followed by an exact revert of the same section is refused, on that
  run and every run after it. The first run's row was retired when the edit
  superseded it, and the bridge will not write text a retired row already
  holds, so the reverted section cannot be stored and the replacement written
  for the edit stays live claiming text the file no longer contains. Reviving a
  retired memory is deliberately not a tool the bridge exposes; whether it
  should be is an open captain decision, and this is a known deferred bound
  rather than an accident.

FAILURE ISOLATION
  A lane whose bridge refuses to start costs that lane's pointers and no
  others: the run continues, and reports exactly which pointers did not land.
  Stopping at the first bad bank would leave how much of the fleet is indexed
  unknowable, which is the failure shape this whole bridge exists to avoid.

COUNTS
  written, skipped_duplicate, updated, expired, refused, expiry_refused.
  Every refusal record carries `kind`: 'write' for a pointer that did not land,
  'expiry' for a stale pointer that could not be retired. They are counted
  apart because they are different failures.

EXIT STATUS
  0 every pointer is durable in the store and everything stale was retired;
  1 anything was refused or unrouted; 2 usage.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import hashlib
import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Any, Dict, Iterable, List, NamedTuple, Optional, Set, Tuple

BRIDGE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / "fm-memory-mcp"
PROJECT_MODE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / "fm-project-mode.sh"
LEDGER_FILENAME = "fm-memory-pointers.json"
SESSION = "fm:memory-pointers"
SOURCE = "firstmate-pointers"
LANE_CHANNEL = "_lane"
CAPTAIN_LANE = "shared"
LEARNINGS_LANE = "fleet-infra"

# How much of a section's body a pointer carries. Enough to act on without
# reopening the file, short enough that 83 of them do not become the bulk
# injection the bridge exists to replace.
EXCERPT_CHARS = 360

# A canonical file that is present and readable but derives nothing is a
# PROBLEM, never a derivation. Truncated mid-write, emptied by accident, or
# restructured out of `## ` headings all look identical to every section having
# been deleted, and retiring on that reading is unrecoverable: the bridge will
# not write text a retired row already holds.
EMPTY_SOURCE = "{path} is readable but derives no pointers"

DECISION_MARKERS = ("captain-decided", "captain-validated", "restated", "conditional")

# `- <name> [<annotation>] - <description> (added <date>)`; the annotation is
# optional and bin/fm-project-mode.sh owns what is inside it.
PROJECT_LINE_RE = re.compile(r"^-\s+(\S+)\s*(\[[^\]]*\])?\s*-?\s*(.*)$")


class PointerError(Exception):
    """A refusal that must never be reported as a successful run."""


# --- deriving the pointer set ------------------------------------------------


def read_lines(path: Path) -> Optional[List[str]]:
    try:
        return path.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return None


def sections(lines: List[str]) -> Iterable[Tuple[int, str, List[str]]]:
    """Every `## ` section as (1-based heading line, heading, body lines)."""
    start: Optional[int] = None
    heading = ""
    body: List[str] = []
    for index, line in enumerate(lines, start=1):
        if line.startswith("## "):
            if start is not None:
                yield (start, heading, body)
            start, heading, body = index, line[3:].strip(), []
        elif start is not None:
            body.append(line)
    if start is not None:
        yield (start, heading, body)


def excerpt(body: List[str]) -> str:
    """A bounded, single-line summary of a section body.

    Markdown bullets and emphasis are flattened rather than dropped: the text
    inside them is the fact, and a pointer that lost it would send the reader
    back to the file for something it could have carried.
    """
    text = " ".join(body)
    text = re.sub(r"`{1,3}", "", text)
    text = re.sub(r"\*\*|__", "", text)
    text = re.sub(r"^\s*[-*]\s+", "", text)
    text = re.sub(r"\s+[-*]\s+", "; ", text)
    text = re.sub(r"\s+", " ", text).strip()
    if len(text) <= EXCERPT_CHARS:
        return text
    cut = text[:EXCERPT_CHARS]
    stop = max(cut.rfind(". "), cut.rfind("; "))
    if stop > EXCERPT_CHARS // 2:
        return cut[: stop + 1].strip()
    return cut.rstrip() + "..."


def digest(*parts: str) -> str:
    """The canonical digest a later drift pass compares against.

    It covers the whole derived pointer text, which is exactly what the store
    dedupes on, so the two agree on what 'changed' means. The line number is
    deliberately outside both: a section that only MOVED says the same thing,
    and rewriting it would retire a row whose text a later revert can never
    write again.
    """
    h = hashlib.sha256()
    for part in parts:
        h.update(part.encode("utf-8"))
        h.update(b"\0")
    return h.hexdigest()


def pointer(
    *,
    lane: str,
    channel_id: str,
    tag: str,
    anchor: str,
    line: int,
    canonical_file: str,
    body_text: str,
    memory_type: str,
    importance: float,
    veracity: str,
) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    summary = excerpt([body_text]) if body_text else ""
    content = f"[{tag}] {anchor}."
    if summary:
        content += f" {summary}" if summary.endswith((".", "!", "?")) else f" {summary}."
    content += f' Canonical: firstmate {canonical_file} § "{anchor}".'
    canonical_sha = digest(canonical_file, anchor, content)
    return {
        "key": f"{canonical_file}#{anchor}",
        "lane": lane,
        "channel_id": channel_id,
        "content": content,
        "memory_type": memory_type,
        "importance": importance,
        "veracity": veracity,
        "canonical_sha": canonical_sha,
        "metadata": {
            "pointer": True,
            "canonical_file": canonical_file,
            "canonical_anchor": anchor,
            "canonical_line": line,
            "canonical_sha": canonical_sha,
        },
    }


def captain_pointers(home: Path) -> Tuple[List[Dict[str, Any]], List[str], bool]:
    lines = read_lines(home / "data" / "captain.md")
    if lines is None:
        return ([], ["data/captain.md is absent"], False)
    out = []
    for line_no, heading, body in sections(lines):
        decided = any(marker in heading.lower() for marker in DECISION_MARKERS)
        out.append(
            pointer(
                lane=CAPTAIN_LANE,
                channel_id=LANE_CHANNEL,
                tag="captain-decision" if decided else "captain-preference",
                anchor=heading,
                line=line_no,
                canonical_file="data/captain.md",
                body_text=" ".join(body),
                memory_type="decision" if decided else "preference",
                importance=0.9 if decided else 0.85,
                veracity="stated",
            )
        )
    return (out, [], True)


def learning_pointers(home: Path) -> Tuple[List[Dict[str, Any]], List[str], bool]:
    lines = read_lines(home / "data" / "learnings.md")
    if lines is None:
        return ([], ["data/learnings.md is absent"], False)
    out = []
    for line_no, heading, body in sections(lines):
        out.append(
            pointer(
                lane=LEARNINGS_LANE,
                channel_id=LANE_CHANNEL,
                tag="fleet-learning",
                anchor=heading,
                line=line_no,
                canonical_file="data/learnings.md",
                body_text=" ".join(body),
                memory_type="learning",
                importance=0.6,
                veracity="tool",
            )
        )
    return (out, [], True)


def project_lane(home: Path, project: str) -> Optional[str]:
    """The project's lane, from the script that owns the registry line format."""
    result = subprocess.run(  # noqa: S603
        [str(PROJECT_MODE), "--lane", project],
        env=dict(os.environ, FM_HOME=str(home)),
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        check=False,
    )
    lane = result.stdout.strip()
    return lane if result.returncode == 0 and lane else None


def project_pointers(home: Path) -> Tuple[List[Dict[str, Any]], List[str], bool]:
    lines = read_lines(home / "data" / "projects.md")
    if lines is None:
        return ([], ["data/projects.md is absent"], False)
    out: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []
    unrouted: List[str] = []
    for line_no, line in enumerate(lines, start=1):
        match = PROJECT_LINE_RE.match(line)
        if not match or line.startswith("- ["):
            continue
        name, annotation, description = match.group(1), match.group(2) or "", match.group(3)
        lane = project_lane(home, name)
        if lane is None:
            unrouted.append(name)
            continue
        # The lane token routes this pointer; repeating it inside the pointer
        # is noise, and it would churn the digest on a pure re-lane.
        posture = " ".join(t for t in annotation.strip("[]").split() if not t.startswith("lane:"))
        body = description.strip()
        if posture:
            body = f"Registered posture: {posture}. {body}"
        out.append(
            pointer(
                lane=lane,
                channel_id=name,
                tag="project",
                anchor=name,
                line=line_no,
                canonical_file="data/projects.md",
                body_text=body,
                memory_type="context",
                importance=0.7,
                veracity="stated",
            )
        )
    return (out, [f"{name} carries no lane in data/projects.md" for name in unrouted], True)


def disambiguate_keys(pointers: List[Dict[str, Any]]) -> None:
    """Give every pointer a ledger key of its own.

    Two `## ` sections can carry the same heading, and the ledger is keyed by
    it, so without this the second section reads the first's recorded row as
    its own and supersedes it - retiring one of two live facts on every run.
    The first occurrence keeps the plain key so existing ledgers still match;
    each repeat is numbered by its order in the canonical file.
    """
    seen: Dict[str, int] = {}
    for entry in pointers:
        key = entry["key"]
        occurrence = seen.get(key, 0) + 1
        seen[key] = occurrence
        if occurrence > 1:
            entry["key"] = f"{key}#{occurrence}"


class Derivation(NamedTuple):
    """One pass over the canonical files, and what it could not account for.

    `troubled` names the sources whose derivation came up short, so a lane can
    refuse to retire THOSE keys while still reconciling everything the other
    sources say. `empty` names sources that read fine and produced nothing,
    which is only dangerous once a ledger holds keys for them.
    """

    pointers: List[Dict[str, Any]]
    problems: List[str]
    troubled: List[str]
    empty: List[str]


SOURCES = (
    ("data/captain.md", captain_pointers),
    ("data/learnings.md", learning_pointers),
    ("data/projects.md", project_pointers),
)


def key_source(key: str) -> str:
    """The canonical file a ledger key came from."""
    return key.split("#", 1)[0]


def derive(home: Path) -> Derivation:
    pointers: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []
    problems: List[str] = []
    troubled: List[str] = []
    empty: List[str] = []
    for path, producer in SOURCES:
        found, trouble, readable = producer(home)
        pointers.extend(found)
        problems.extend(trouble)
        if trouble:
            troubled.append(path)
        elif readable and not found:
            empty.append(path)
    disambiguate_keys(pointers)
    return Derivation(pointers, problems, troubled, empty)


# --- the ledger --------------------------------------------------------------


def ledger_path(data_dir: Path, lane: str) -> Path:
    return data_dir / "banks" / f"lane-{lane}" / LEDGER_FILENAME


def read_ledger(data_dir: Path, lane: str) -> Tuple[Dict[str, Any], Optional[str]]:
    """(the pointer records, the home they were derived from).

    The home comes back because reconciliation retires what a derivation no
    longer produces, and a derivation from ANOTHER tree does not produce this
    one's pointers either. A ledger written before the home was recorded reads
    as unbound, which is adopted rather than acted on.
    """
    try:
        loaded = json.loads(ledger_path(data_dir, lane).read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return ({}, None)
    if not isinstance(loaded, dict):
        return ({}, None)
    records = loaded.get("pointers")
    if isinstance(records, dict):
        recorded = loaded.get("home")
        return (records, recorded if isinstance(recorded, str) else None)
    return (loaded, None)


def write_ledger(data_dir: Path, lane: str, ledger: Dict[str, Any], home: Optional[str]) -> None:
    path = ledger_path(data_dir, lane)
    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    payload = {"home": home, "pointers": ledger}
    path.write_text(json.dumps(payload, indent=2, sort_keys=True) + "\n", encoding="utf-8")


# --- speaking to the bridge --------------------------------------------------


class BridgeSession:
    """One `fm-memory-mcp serve` process for one lane, over its own stdio."""

    def __init__(self, lane: str, data_dir: Path, home: Path) -> None:
        self.lane = lane
        self._next_id = 1
        self.proc = subprocess.Popen(  # noqa: S603
            [sys.executable, str(BRIDGE), "serve", "--lane", lane,
             "--data-dir", str(data_dir), "--session", SESSION],
            stdin=subprocess.PIPE,
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
            stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
            env=dict(os.environ, FM_HOME=str(home)),
            text=True,
            bufsize=1,
        )
        try:
            self._call_raw("initialize", {})
        except BaseException:
            self.close()
            raise

    def _call_raw(self, method: str, params: Dict[str, Any]) -> Dict[str, Any]:
        assert self.proc.stdin and self.proc.stdout
        request = {"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": self._next_id, "method": method, "params": params}
        self._next_id += 1
        try:
            self.proc.stdin.write(json.dumps(request) + "\n")
            self.proc.stdin.flush()
            line = self.proc.stdout.readline()
        except (BrokenPipeError, ValueError) as exc:
            raise PointerError(f"lane {self.lane}: the bridge closed its pipe: {exc}") from exc
        if not line:
            stderr = (self.proc.stderr.read() if self.proc.stderr else "").strip()
            raise PointerError(f"lane {self.lane}: the bridge answered nothing. {stderr}")
        try:
            return json.loads(line)
        except ValueError as exc:
            raise PointerError(f"lane {self.lane}: the bridge answered unparseable JSON: {exc}") from exc

    def call_tool(self, name: str, arguments: Dict[str, Any]) -> Tuple[bool, Dict[str, Any]]:
        """(ok, payload) - a typed refusal is data, never an exception."""
        response = self._call_raw("tools/call", {"name": name, "arguments": arguments})
        if "result" not in response:
            raise PointerError(f"lane {self.lane}: {json.dumps(response.get('error'))}")
        result = response["result"]
        try:
            payload = json.loads(result["content"][0]["text"])
        except (ValueError, KeyError, IndexError, TypeError) as exc:
            raise PointerError(f"lane {self.lane}: unreadable {name} result: {exc}") from exc
        # Every caller reads the payload as an object; a list or a bare string
        # is refused here rather than raising an AttributeError past the guards
        # that keep one lane's failure from costing the run.
        if not isinstance(payload, dict):
            raise PointerError(
                f"lane {self.lane}: the bridge answered {name} with "
                f"{type(payload).__name__}, not an object")
        return (not result.get("isError", False), payload)

    def close(self) -> None:
        """Tear the bridge down. Never raises: a dead bridge costs its lane.

        Closing the pipe onto a process that has already gone raises a second
        time on the buffered request its flush could not deliver, and letting
        that escape would lose the counts for every pointer that did land.
        """
        try:
            self._call_raw("shutdown", {})
        except (PointerError, ValueError):
            pass
        finally:
            for stream in (self.proc.stdin, self.proc.stdout, self.proc.stderr):
                if stream is not None:
                    try:
                        stream.close()
                    except OSError:
                        pass
            try:
                self.proc.wait(timeout=30)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                self.proc.kill()
                try:
                    self.proc.wait(timeout=30)
                except (subprocess.TimeoutExpired, OSError):
                    pass
            except OSError:
                pass


def write_arguments(entry: Dict[str, Any]) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    args = {
        "content": entry["content"],
        "memory_type": entry["memory_type"],
        "importance": entry["importance"],
        "veracity": entry["veracity"],
        "scope": "global",
        "source": SOURCE,
        "metadata": entry["metadata"],
    }
    if entry["channel_id"] != LANE_CHANNEL:
        args["project"] = entry["channel_id"]
    return args


def stale_keys(ledger: Dict[str, Any], current_keys: Iterable[str]) -> List[str]:
    """Ledger keys this derivation no longer produces."""
    current = set(current_keys)
    return [key for key in sorted(ledger) if key not in current]


def retirable_keys(ledger: Dict[str, Any], current_keys: Iterable[str]) -> List[str]:
    """Stale keys naming a row that only an expiry can retire.

    A key with no memory id names nothing, and a key whose row this run rebound
    to a surviving pointer names the survivor. Neither is a pointer left stale,
    so neither belongs in a count of what reconciliation could not retire.
    """
    current = set(current_keys)
    live_ids = {
        (record or {}).get("memory_id")
        for key, record in ledger.items()
        if key in current
    }
    out = []
    for key in stale_keys(ledger, current):
        memory_id = (ledger.get(key) or {}).get("memory_id")
        if memory_id and memory_id not in live_ids:
            out.append(key)
    return out


def ledger_record(entry: Dict[str, Any], memory_id: str) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    """What the ledger remembers about one pointer that landed."""
    return {
        "memory_id": memory_id,
        "canonical_sha": entry["canonical_sha"],
        "canonical_line": entry["metadata"]["canonical_line"],
    }


def reconciliation_state(recorded_home: Optional[str], home_key: str) -> Tuple[bool, bool, str]:
    """(may retire, may chain onto recorded ids, why not).

    A ledger naming another tree records rows this run did not create, and a
    derivation from another tree does not produce them either. Chaining onto
    them would retire them exactly as irreversibly as expiring them, so a
    foreign ledger is neither retired from nor superseded through: this run
    writes plainly and leaves those rows alone.
    """
    if recorded_home is None:
        return (False, True, "the ledger recorded no home, so it was adopted and nothing was retired")
    if recorded_home != home_key:
        return (False, False, f"the ledger was written from {recorded_home}, not from {home_key}")
    return (True, True, "")


def reconcile_lane(
    session: BridgeSession,
    lane: str,
    ledger: Dict[str, Any],
    keys: List[str],
    counts: Dict[str, int],
    refusals: List[Dict[str, Any]],
    accounted: Set[str],
) -> None:
    """Retire the pointers this derivation no longer produces.

    A renamed heading, a deleted section, and a renumbered repeat all read the
    same way from here: a ledger key the run did not derive. Its row is still
    live and still claims a canonical section that is gone, which is the stale
    knowledge the whole index exists to prevent, so it is expired through the
    bridge.
    """
    for key in keys:
        memory_id = (ledger.get(key) or {}).get("memory_id")
        if not memory_id:
            accounted.add(key)
            ledger.pop(key, None)
            continue
        ok, payload = session.call_tool("memory_expire", {"memory_id": memory_id})
        error = payload.get("error") or {}
        accounted.add(key)
        if ok or error.get("code") == "not_found":
            counts["expired"] += 1
            del ledger[key]
            continue
        counts["expiry_refused"] += 1
        refusals.append({"kind": "expiry", "key": key, "lane": lane, "error": error})


def lane_failure(exc: BaseException) -> Dict[str, str]:
    code = "lane_unavailable" if isinstance(exc, PointerError) else "lane_failed"
    return {"code": code, "message": str(exc) or exc.__class__.__name__}


def ledger_lanes(data_dir: Path) -> List[str]:
    """Every lane whose bank carries a ledger of ours.

    A lane that lost its LAST pointer derives nothing, so walking only the
    derived set would never visit it again and its rows would keep claiming a
    project the registry no longer lanes there. The ledger beside the bank is
    what remembers the lane existed.
    """
    try:
        banks = sorted((data_dir / "banks").iterdir())
    except OSError:
        return []
    return [
        bank.name[len("lane-"):]
        for bank in banks
        if bank.name.startswith("lane-") and (bank / LEDGER_FILENAME).is_file()
    ]


def write_pointers(
    home: Path,
    data_dir: Path,
    pointers: List[Dict[str, Any]],
    troubled: Iterable[str] = (),
    empty: Iterable[str] = (),
) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    """Write every pointer through the bridge, one serve process per lane."""
    by_lane: Dict[str, List[Dict[str, Any]]] = {}
    for entry in pointers:
        by_lane.setdefault(entry["lane"], []).append(entry)
    for lane in ledger_lanes(data_dir):
        by_lane.setdefault(lane, [])

    ledgers = {lane: read_ledger(data_dir, lane) for lane in sorted(by_lane)}
    # A source that read fine and derived nothing is only dangerous once a
    # ledger holds its keys, because that is the case where reconciliation
    # would retire them. An empty data/learnings.md on a home that has never
    # recorded a learning has nothing to lose and is not a problem at all.
    at_risk = sorted({
        key_source(key)
        for records, _ in ledgers.values()
        for key in records
    })
    dangerous = [path for path in sorted(set(empty)) if path in at_risk]
    troubled_sources = set(troubled) | set(dangerous)

    counts = {"written": 0, "skipped_duplicate": 0, "updated": 0,
              "expired": 0, "refused": 0, "expiry_refused": 0}
    refusals: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []
    skipped: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []
    # Compared as a resolved path, so a trailing slash or a symlinked tmp root
    # is not read as a different tree.
    home_key = str(home.resolve())
    for lane in sorted(by_lane):
        ledger, recorded_home = ledgers[lane]
        reconciling, chaining, reason = reconciliation_state(recorded_home, home_key)
        # A ledger bound to another tree is left exactly as its owner wrote it.
        # Rebinding it would retire this home's absent keys on the very next
        # run, and rewriting its records would point the owner's reconciliation
        # at rows this run created, so its next stale key would retire a
        # pointer it never wrote. This run writes plainly and records nothing.
        owns_ledger = recorded_home in (None, home_key)
        entries = by_lane[lane]
        current_keys = [entry["key"] for entry in entries]
        stale = stale_keys(ledger, current_keys)
        # A source that came up short cannot say anything was deleted, so its
        # keys are left exactly as they are while the rest of the lane
        # reconciles normally.
        guarded = [key for key in stale if key_source(key) in troubled_sources]
        guarded_keys = set(guarded)
        reconcilable = [key for key in stale if key not in guarded_keys]

        def leave_alone(keys: List[str], why: str) -> None:
            if keys:
                skipped.append({"lane": lane, "keys": keys, "reason": why})

        if guarded:
            leave_alone(guarded, "the canonical source that owns these keys derived nothing")
        if not reconciling:
            # A foreign ledger is reported even with nothing stale in it: this
            # run wrote past rows it does not own and reconciled none of them,
            # which is not the same outcome as a run that reconciled the lane.
            if reconcilable or (ledger and not chaining):
                skipped.append({"lane": lane, "keys": reconcilable, "reason": reason})
            reconcilable = []
        retirable_now = set(retirable_keys(ledger, current_keys))
        retirable = [key for key in reconcilable if key in retirable_now]

        if not entries and not reconcilable:
            if ledger and owns_ledger:
                write_ledger(data_dir, lane, ledger, home_key)
            continue
        # A lane whose bridge will not start - an unprovisioned or wiped bank -
        # costs that lane's pointers and nothing else. Stopping the whole index
        # would make one missing bank hide how much of the fleet is indexed,
        # and the run has to report exactly which pointers did not land.
        try:
            session = BridgeSession(lane, data_dir, home)
        except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001
            counts["refused"] += len(entries)
            if entries:
                refusals.append({
                    "kind": "write", "lane": lane, "keys": current_keys,
                    "error": lane_failure(exc),
                })
            counts["expiry_refused"] += len(retirable)
            if retirable:
                refusals.append({
                    "kind": "expiry", "lane": lane, "keys": retirable,
                    "error": lane_failure(exc),
                })
            continue
        try:
            walked = True
            for index, entry in enumerate(entries):
                recorded = ledger.get(entry["key"]) or {}
                args = write_arguments(entry)
                superseding = bool(
                    chaining
                    and recorded.get("memory_id")
                    and recorded.get("canonical_sha")
                    and recorded["canonical_sha"] != entry["canonical_sha"]
                )
                # A bridge that dies mid-lane costs the rest of THIS lane and no
                # other: the remaining keys are reported as refused and the walk
                # moves on, because a run that aborts here loses the counts for
                # every pointer that already landed. Anything unexpected is
                # treated the same way, for the same reason.
                try:
                    payload = {}
                    superseded = False
                    if superseding:
                        ok, payload = session.call_tool(
                            "memory_supersede", dict(args, memory_id=recorded["memory_id"]))
                        superseded = ok
                        # A refusal means the recorded row is gone or already
                        # retired, so there is nothing to chain onto and the
                        # pointer is written on its own.
                    if not superseded:
                        ok, payload = session.call_tool("memory_remember", args)
                except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001
                    stranded = entries[index:]
                    counts["refused"] += len(stranded)
                    refusals.append({
                        "kind": "write", "lane": lane,
                        "keys": [item["key"] for item in stranded],
                        "error": lane_failure(exc),
                    })
                    # The expiry half of this lane is lost with the same bridge,
                    # so it is reported here rather than left unaccounted.
                    counts["expiry_refused"] += len(retirable)
                    if retirable:
                        refusals.append({
                            "kind": "expiry", "lane": lane, "keys": retirable,
                            "error": lane_failure(exc),
                        })
                    walked = False
                    break
                # A result the bridge called successful still has to name the
                # row it landed on: without an id there is nothing to record,
                # and guessing one would point the ledger at nothing.
                memory_id = payload.get("id") if ok else None
                if ok and memory_id and superseded:
                    counts["updated"] += 1
                    if owns_ledger:
                        ledger[entry["key"]] = ledger_record(entry, memory_id)
                    continue
                if not ok or not memory_id:
                    counts["refused"] += 1
                    refusals.append({
                        "kind": "write",
                        "key": entry["key"],
                        "lane": lane,
                        "error": payload.get("error") or {
                            "code": "malformed_result",
                            "message": f"lane {lane}: the bridge reported success with no memory id",
                        },
                    })
                    continue
                counts["written" if payload.get("created") else "skipped_duplicate"] += 1
                if owns_ledger:
                    ledger[entry["key"]] = ledger_record(entry, memory_id)
            if walked and reconcilable:
                # Only once the walk finished: a lane that lost its bridge has
                # nothing to expire through, and its unreconciled keys have to
                # stay in the ledger or the ids needed to retire them later are
                # stranded with no way back. Recomputed here because the walk
                # may have rebound a recorded row onto a surviving pointer.
                accounted: Set[str] = set()
                allowed = set(reconcilable)
                pending = [key for key in retirable_keys(ledger, current_keys) if key in allowed]
                pending_keys = set(pending)
                for key in [key for key in reconcilable if key not in pending_keys]:
                    ledger.pop(key, None)
                try:
                    reconcile_lane(session, lane, ledger, pending, counts, refusals, accounted)
                except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001
                    # Only the keys reconciliation never reached: one already
                    # reported as refused is not stale twice.
                    stranded = [key for key in pending if key not in accounted]
                    counts["expiry_refused"] += len(stranded)
                    if stranded:
                        refusals.append({
                            "kind": "expiry", "lane": lane, "keys": stranded,
                            "error": lane_failure(exc),
                        })
        finally:
            session.close()
            if owns_ledger:
                write_ledger(data_dir, lane, ledger, home_key)
    result: Dict[str, Any] = {"counts": counts, "refusals": refusals}
    if skipped:
        result["skipped_reconciliation"] = skipped
    if dangerous:
        result["problems"] = [EMPTY_SOURCE.format(path=path) for path in dangerous]
    return result


# --- CLI ---------------------------------------------------------------------


def lane_summary(pointers: List[Dict[str, Any]]) -> Dict[str, int]:
    summary: Dict[str, int] = {}
    for entry in pointers:
        summary[entry["lane"]] = summary.get(entry["lane"], 0) + 1
    return dict(sorted(summary.items()))


def main(argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="fm-memory-pointers",
        description=__doc__,
        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter,
    )
    parser.add_argument("command", choices=["plan", "write"])
    parser.add_argument("--home", help="firstmate home holding data/ (default: FM_HOME, then this repo)")
    parser.add_argument("--data-dir", help="Mnemosyne data dir (default: the bridge's own default)")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)

    home = Path(args.home or os.environ.get("FM_HOME") or Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent)
    derived = derive(home)
    pointers, problems = derived.pointers, list(derived.problems)

    if args.command == "plan":
        print(json.dumps({
            "status": "error" if problems else "ok",
            "home": str(home),
            "total": len(pointers),
            "lanes": lane_summary(pointers),
            "problems": problems,
            "pointers": pointers,
        }, indent=2))
        return 1 if problems else 0

    if not pointers:
        print(json.dumps({"status": "error", "home": str(home),
                          "problems": problems or ["no canonical sections found"]}, indent=2))
        return 1

    data_dir = Path(args.data_dir) if args.data_dir else None
    if data_dir is None:
        raw = os.environ.get("MNEMOSYNE_DATA_DIR")
        data_dir = Path(raw).expanduser() if raw else Path.home() / ".hermes" / "mnemosyne" / "data"
    try:
        result = write_pointers(home, data_dir, pointers, derived.troubled, derived.empty)
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001
        print(json.dumps({"status": "error", "error": str(exc) or exc.__class__.__name__}, indent=2))
        return 1
    # A source that derived nothing only becomes a problem once a ledger holds
    # its keys, which only the run can know.
    problems.extend(result.get("problems", []))
    # A run that deliberately did less than it was asked must never look like
    # one that did everything.
    failed = (
        bool(problems)
        or result["counts"]["refused"]
        or result["counts"]["expiry_refused"]
        or bool(result.get("skipped_reconciliation"))
    )
    report = {
        "status": "error" if failed else "ok",
        "home": str(home),
        "data_dir": str(data_dir),
        "total": len(pointers),
        "lanes": lane_summary(pointers),
        "counts": result["counts"],
        "refusals": result["refusals"],
        "problems": problems,
    }
    if "skipped_reconciliation" in result:
        report["skipped_reconciliation"] = result["skipped_reconciliation"]
    print(json.dumps(report, indent=2))
    return 1 if failed else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
